{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Read only the provided context files needed for this page generation.\ncontrols_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/config/discussion_controls.json')\ntopics_dir = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/topics')\n\ncontrols = json.loads(controls_path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\nallowed_personas = controls.get('allowed_persona_ids') or controls.get('persona_ids') or [\n    'persona-maya-santoro',\n    'persona-eli-okafor',\n    'persona-lina-kovac',\n    'persona-rory-chen',\n    'persona-samira-haddad',\n]\n\n# Narrowly read the matching topic file if present; task_context already supplies the page payload.\ntopic_id = 'topic-near-term-demonstrations-versus-fault-tolerant-systems'\nfor candidate in (\n    topics_dir / f'{topic_id}.json',\n    topics_dir / f'{topic_id}.md',\n):\n    if candidate.exists():\n        candidate.read_text(encoding='utf-8')\n        break\n\noutput_json_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/runs/run-20260426T201248Z--aqilsezfv4/semantic/comment_section_generation/topic--topic-near-term-demonstrations-versus-fault-tolerant-systems.json')\noutput_json_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\ncomments = [\n    {\n        'comment_ref': 'draft-1',\n        'persona_id': 'persona-maya-santoro',\n        'body': 'This page is strongest when it separates three different meanings of progress. Sycamore shows a real task-specific sampling result, Preskill gives a disciplined case for NISQ-era scientific experiments, and the quantum-internet work is still closer to a roadmap of primitives than a deployed scalable network.',\n        'parent_ref': None,\n        'score_assessment': {\n            'score': 24,\n            'rationale': 'Accurately distinguishes demonstration, testbed, and roadmap evidence across the sources.'\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        'comment_ref': 'draft-2',\n        'persona_id': 'persona-eli-okafor',\n        'body': 'The optimization caveat matters because near-term quantum advantage can sound broader than the evidence. QAOA and VQE are valuable experimental probes, but beating decades of classical optimization or chemistry methods is a much stronger claim than running an interesting noisy circuit.',\n        'parent_ref': None,\n        'score_assessment': {\n            'score': 23,\n            'rationale': 'Source-grounded caution about NISQ algorithms and classical benchmark standards.'\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        'comment_ref': 'draft-3',\n        'persona_id': 'persona-lina-kovac',\n        'body': 'I agree, and the same boundary shows up in the Sycamore result. The 53-qubit random-circuit experiment is important because it made a narrow sampling task classically hard at comparable fidelity, not because it removed the need for logical qubits, error correction, or application-specific algorithms.',\n        'parent_ref': 'draft-2',\n        'score_assessment': {\n            'score': 25,\n            'rationale': 'Strong reply that links the parent caveat to the concrete Sycamore benchmark limits.'\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        'comment_ref': 'draft-4',\n        'persona_id': 'persona-rory-chen',\n        'body': 'The quantum-network examples make the page feel less computer-centric in a useful way. Stored heralded entanglement, retrieval efficiency, and early swapping are genuine milestones, but Kimble still frames repeaters, teleportation, local processing, and scalable verification as unsolved system-level requirements.',\n        'parent_ref': None,\n        'score_assessment': {\n            'score': 22,\n            'rationale': 'Broadens the discussion with accurate evidence from the quantum-internet source.'\n        }\n    },\n    {\n        'comment_ref': 'draft-5',\n        'persona_id': 'persona-samira-haddad',\n        'body': 'A useful takeaway is that near-term devices can be scientifically serious without being fault tolerant. That middle category keeps both mistakes out of the discussion: dismissing noisy demonstrations as irrelevant, or treating every hard-to-simulate experiment as general-purpose quantum computing.',\n        'parent_ref': None,\n        'score_assessment': {\n            'score': 24,\n            'rationale': 'Balanced synthesis that matches the page thesis and avoids overclaiming.'\n        }\n    },\n]\n\n# Ensure only allowed personas are emitted.\nallowed = set(allowed_personas)\nfor comment in comments:\n    if comment['persona_id'] not in allowed:\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Persona not allowed: {comment['persona_id']}\\\")\n\npayload = {\n    'page_ref': 'topic:topic-near-term-demonstrations-versus-fault-tolerant-systems',\n    'requested_count': 5,\n    'comments': comments,\n}\n\noutput_json_path.write_text(json.dumps(payload, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + '\\\\n', encoding='utf-8')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
